 {"id":"5.33","q":"According to the book, secular trends occur about once in:","o":["7 to 10 years","2 to 6 years","Every year","Every quarter"],"a":0,"w":"Cyclical trends run in 2 to 6 year cycles; seasonal trends are annual."},
 {"id":"5.34","q":"Which trend changes what is consumed or how it is produced, rather than just how much?","o":["Secular trend","Cyclical trend","Seasonal trend","Inventory cycle"],"a":0,"w":"Cyclical trends change quantity; secular trends cause displacement."},
 {"id":"5.35","q":"In which phase of the economic cycle do interest rates and inflation both tend to fall?","o":["Recession","Expansion","Slowdown","Peak"],"a":0,"w":"People save instead of borrowing, so rates fall; lower consumption brings inflation down."},
 {"id":"5.36","q":"In the recovery phase, what allows the central bank to loosen monetary policy?","o":["A low inflation rate","A high fiscal deficit","Rising unemployment only","A strong currency"],"a":0,"w":"The book says low inflation enables central banks to extend liquidity."},
 {"id":"5.37","q":"Short-term cycles that occur within a commodity cycle because of stock adjustments by producers and customers are called:","o":["Inventory cycles","Secular trends","Economic cycles","Seasonal trends"],"a":0,"w":"The book's example: crude oil inventory in 2020."},
 {"id":"5.38","q":"To account for seasonality in a simple way, analysts compare a quarter's results with:","o":["The same quarter of the previous year","The immediately preceding quarter","The industry average","The company's forecast"],"a":0,"w":"Year-over-year comparison keeps the season the same on both sides."},
 {"id":"5.39","q":"Which of the following is NOT listed in the book as a source of information for economic analysis?","o":["Company's internal board minutes","Economic Survey","Websites of regulators such as SEBI and RBI","Published economic research reports"],"a":0,"w":"Board minutes are company documents, and not public. The book lists government sites, regulators, research reports and the Economic Survey."},
